## Claim
Text: "You see the number today? 33.1 GDP. The biggest in the history of our country by almost triple, right? Almost triple. Now it’s very much bigger than any GDP we’ve ever had. You have to go back to the 1950s, and then it’s less than half. This is the greatest number, 33.1 percent."
Claim author: Donald Trump
Claim date: October 30, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### What specific economic metric (e.g., real GDP growth rate annualized) did Donald Trump refer to when he said "33.1 GDP" on October 30, 2020?
Answer: Donald Trump was referring to the **annualized real GDP growth rate** for the third quarter of 2020, which the Bureau of Economic Analysis reported as 33.1% at a seasonally adjusted annual rate (SAAR). As the Politifact article explains, this figure represents the quarterly growth of 7.4% extrapolated over a full year: "If that lasted for a year, it would add up to a growth rate of 33.1%."

Source URL: https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2020/oct/30/donald-trump/donald-trump-says-gdp-growth-was-historic-beat-pre/

### According to official U.S. government statistics, what was the real GDP growth rate (annualized) for the third quarter of 2020?
Answer: According to the Bureau of Economic Analysis's advance estimate, the real GDP growth rate (annualized) for the third quarter of 2020 was 33.1 percent.

Source URL: https://www.bea.gov/news/2020/gross-domestic-product-third-quarter-2020-advance-estimate

### What was the highest real GDP growth rate (annualized) recorded in U.S. history prior to the third quarter of 2020, and what was that value?
Answer: The highest real GDP growth rate (annualized) recorded in U.S. history prior to the third quarter of 2020 was 16.7 percent at an annual rate, which corresponded to a quarterly growth rate of 3.9 percent, set in the first quarter of 1950.

Source URL: https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/articles/great-american-recovery-third-quarter-gdp-blows-past-expectations/

### Is the real GDP growth rate of 33.1% for Q3 2020 the highest in U.S. history?
Answer: Yes, the annualized real GDP growth rate of 33.1% for Q3 2020 is the highest in U.S. history, based on the source's comparison: “Sustained annual growth at this rate would translate to a 33.1% increase in GDP over the year. For comparison, GDP grew by 2.2% in 2019 and a record 18.9% in 1942.” This indicates that 33.1% surpasses the previous record annual growth of 18.9%.

Source URL: https://usafacts.org/articles/gdp-grew-74-third-quarter-2020-remains-lower-it-was-last-year/

### Was the Q3 2020 GDP growth rate almost triple (i.e., approximately three times) the previous record GDP growth rate?
Answer: No, the Q3 2020 GDP growth rate was not almost triple the previous record; according to the source, it was "roughly twice the prior record" set in the first quarter of 1950.

Source URL: https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/articles/great-american-recovery-third-quarter-gdp-blows-past-expectations/

### Is it true that the highest GDP growth rate from the 1950s was less than half of the 33.1% Q3 2020 growth rate?
Answer: Yes, it is true. According to the source, the prior record for GDP growth was 16.7% at an annual rate in the first quarter of 1950, which is "roughly twice" the prior record but indeed less than half of the 33.1% annualized growth rate recorded in Q3 2020.

Source URL: https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/articles/great-american-recovery-third-quarter-gdp-blows-past-expectations/

### Does the claim that "you have to go back to the 1950s, and then it's less than half" imply that the Q3 2020 growth rate is more than double any 1950s growth rate?
Answer: Yes, the claim implies that the Q3 2020 growth rate is more than double any 1950s growth rate, because stating that a 1950s rate is "less than half" of the current rate logically means the current rate is more than twice that 1950s rate. However, the search result shows that the highest quarterly growth in the 1950s was 3.9% (Q1 1950), while Q3 2020 quarterly growth was 7.4%—meaning 3.9% is actually slightly more than half of 7.4% (half is 3.7%), so the claim's premise is not supported by the data.

Source URL: https://usafacts.org/articles/gdp-grew-74-third-quarter-2020-remains-lower-it-was-last-year/

### Does the fact that the Q3 2020 GDP growth rate was a rebound from the COVID-19 recession affect the interpretation of it being the "biggest in history"?
Answer: Yes, the fact that the Q3 2020 GDP growth rate was a rebound from the COVID-19 recession significantly affects its interpretation as the "biggest in history." The 33.1% annualized figure is a record largely because it follows an unprecedented 10% non-annualized plunge in the first half of the year, meaning the economy was "clawing back much of the ground it had lost" rather than achieving new overall output levels. As the article notes, "the level of GDP [remains] 3.5% lower than in Q4 last year, before Covid struck," so the headline number reflects a statistical bounce from a deep trough, not a sign of robust, sustained growth.

Source URL: https://www.cbsnews.com/news/gdp-q3-growth-33-1-percent/

### What is the official source (e.g., Bureau of Economic Analysis) that reported the 33.1% GDP growth rate for Q3 2020?
Answer: The official source that reported the 33.1% GDP growth rate for Q3 2020 is the Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA), as stated in its advance estimate news release: "Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 33.1 percent in the third quarter of 2020... according to the 'advance' estimate released by the Bureau of Economic Analysis."

Source URL: https://www.bea.gov/news/2020/gross-domestic-product-third-quarter-2020-advance-estimate

## Final Judgement
### Step 1: Summary of key insights
The claim refers to the annualized real GDP growth rate of 33.1% for Q3 2020, which is indeed the highest such figure in U.S. history. However, the claim exaggerates the margin: the previous record (16.7% in Q1 1950) is roughly double, not “almost triple,” and the 1950s rate is actually slightly more than half of 33.1%, not “less than half.” Moreover, the record growth was a rebound from the COVID-19 recession, and the overall level of GDP remained below pre-pandemic levels—context that the claim omits, making the statistic appear to reflect robust, sustained growth rather than a statistical bounce.

### Step 2: Information still lacking
No further information is needed; the evidence is sufficient to evaluate the claim.

### Step 3: Conclusion
The claim is technically true in that the 33.1% annualized growth rate is the highest ever recorded, but it misleads by exaggerating the comparison (claiming “almost triple” when it is roughly double) and by omitting the crucial context that this growth was a recovery from a deep recession, not a sign of sustained economic strength. Including that context would significantly alter the impression of the claim. Therefore, the appropriate decision is `cherry-picking`.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim that the 33.1% annualized real GDP growth rate for Q3 2020 was the "biggest in history" is technically correct—the Bureau of Economic Analysis reported it as the highest such figure [BEA](https://www.bea.gov/news/2020/gross-domestic-product-third-quarter-2020-advance-estimate). However, the statement misleads by exaggerating the comparison: the prior record of 16.7% (Q1 1950) is roughly double, not "almost triple" [White House archive](https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/articles/great-american-recovery-third-quarter-gdp-blows-past-expectations/), and the 1950s rate is slightly more than half of 33.1%, not "less than half" [USAFacts](https://usafacts.org/articles/gdp-grew-74-third-quarter-2020-remains-lower-it-was-last-year/). Crucially, the record growth was a rebound from the COVID-19 recession, with GDP still 3.5% below pre-pandemic levels, making the headline a statistical bounce rather than a sign of sustained strength [CBS News](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/gdp-q3-growth-33-1-percent/). This omission of context constitutes cherry-picking.